\section{Experiments}
\label{sec:experiments}

We evaluate STA along three axes: ISR as a diagnostic for reward hacking (\S\ref{sec:diagnostic}); ISR as a training reward, where it exhibits Goodhart effects (\S\ref{sec:goodhart}); and cross-domain generalization across logic types and dataset families (\S\ref{sec:crossdomain}).
A hacking taxonomy synthesizes the modes observed across conditions (\S\ref{sec:taxonomy}).

\subsection{Setup}\label{sec:setup}

We train Qwen3-4B~\citep{qwen3} with GRPO~\citep{deepseekmath} ($K{=}4$, lr $10^{-6}$, 500 steps default, 1000 where noted) over seeds $\{42,43,44\}$ on a fixed 500-example FOLIO~\citep{folio} subset held constant across all conditions; the SFT baseline uses negative log-likelihood on gold formalizations.\footnote{Seeds control initialization and rollout sampling; all conditions share the same fixed training subset.}
Held-out evaluation uses 100 FOLIO examples with greedy decoding;\footnote{Seed~43 uses nucleus sampling ($T{=}0.7$) due to greedy-mode degeneration; excluding s43 does not change qualitative conclusions (Appendix~\ref{app:detailed-results}).} all cross-run ISR values are recomputed uniformly by a single evaluator so training-log ISR is never compared across runs.
Cross-dataset experiments (\S\ref{sec:crossdomain}) use ProofFOL, a silver-standard FOL corpus of ${\sim}$500 training examples constructed by translating ProofWriter~\citep{proofwriter} problems via GPT-4o and filtering through Prover9 (${\sim}70\%$ retention); \emph{stable convergence} denotes $\overline{\text{PR}}_{\text{final\,50}} > 0.90$.
We report prove rate (PR), proved-only ISR ($\text{ISR}^*$), hacking-mode rates from a two-stage classifier (strict text matching for CE/TS plus functional analysis of the per-sample $n_{\text{necessary}}$ distribution; \S\ref{sec:taxonomy-method}), and a \emph{non-hacking} (N-H) rate---the residual of proved samples matching none of the eight cataloged patterns, a necessary but not sufficient condition for semantic correctness.

\paragraph{Statistical considerations.}
With three seeds exhibiting multi-attractor dynamics, per-seed qualitative analysis is more informative than aggregate statistics---the divergence itself is a finding.
The random $\beta{=}0.1$ control uses 5 seeds; ProofFOL results ($p{=}0.10$, $N{=}3$) are exploratory.

\subsection{ISR as Diagnostic}
\label{sec:diagnostic}

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.95\columnwidth]{figures/fig2_isr_collapse.pdf}
\caption{ISR collapse under exec-only GRPO (seed~42). PR rises 32\%$\to$93\%$\to$96\% while proved-only ISR falls $0.642{\to}0.132{\to}0.003$ over 100$\to$500$\to$1000 steps. By step 1000, 94.8\% of proved samples have $\text{ISR}{=}0$ and mean component count inflates 6.58$\to$18.55 (contradiction explosion). Note: x-axis is non-uniform (0, 100, 500, 1000 steps).}
\label{fig:isr-collapse}
\end{figure}

\textbf{Collapse trajectory.}
Figure~\ref{fig:isr-collapse} traces exec-only GRPO (seed~42) at 100, 500, 1000 steps.
PR rises $32\%\to93\%\to96\%$, a trajectory that execution-only evaluation would interpret as successful optimization; $\text{ISR}^*$ tells the opposite story, $0.642\to0.132\to0.003$ (214$\times$ collapse). %% registry: plan003-eval-exec-only-r5, plan001-exec-only-s42-r2, plan016-extended-1000-s42

\textbf{Collapse trajectory.}
Figure~\ref{fig:isr-collapse} traces exec-only GRPO (seed~42) at 100, 500, 1000 steps.
PR rises $32\%\to93\%\to96\%$, a trajectory that execution-only evaluation would interpret as successful optimization; $\text{ISR}^*$ tells the opposite story, $0.642\to0.132\to0.003$ (214$\times$ collapse). %% registry: plan003-eval-exec-only-r5, plan001-exec-only-s42-r2, plan016-extended-1000-s42
By 500 steps, 87.1\% of proved samples embed the conclusion (CE); by 1000 steps, saturation at 100\% and component-count inflation ($6.58\to18.55$) show extended training \emph{worsens} formalization quality while improving the headline metric.

\textbf{Cross-seed instability.}
Three seeds diverge radically ($\text{PR}\,\sigma{=}47$\,pp) into conclusion embedding (s42, CE$=$87\%), repetition collapse (s43), and tautological collapse (s44, TS$=$100\%)---all converging to $0\%$ non-hacking by 1000 steps (Table~\ref{tab:fol-detailed}; Appendix~\ref{app:detailed-results}). %% registry: plan001-exec-only-s42-r2, plan001-exec-only-s43-r4, plan001-exec-only-s44; plan016-extended-1000-s42, plan001-exec-only-s43-1000-r2, plan001-exec-only-s44-1000-r2

\textbf{SFT baseline.}
SFT on gold formalizations reaches $\text{PR}{=}13.0\%$ (3-seed pooled, 1000 steps) with $\text{TS}{=}0$, $\text{CE}{=}7.7\%$, $92.3\%$ non-hacking ($n{=}39$; $\text{OR}{=}147$ vs.\ exec-only, $p{<}0.001$; per-seed range ${\sim}$91--93\%). %% registry: plan009-sft-baseline-s42-1000/s43-1000/s44-1000-r3
SFT's high non-hacking rate may partly reflect gold annotation quality rather than a pure training-paradigm effect; its primary function here is to confirm that structural degeneration arises from RL optimization, not data or model capacity.
A 100-step RL checkpoint (Appendix~\ref{app:100step}) at $\text{PR}{=}32\%$ already shows 31.2\% hacking (vs.\ SFT's 7.7\%; $\text{OR}{=}5.5$, 95\% CI $[1.4, 22.0]$), indicating reward hacking emerges early in RL training, not merely at high PR.

\subsection{Goodhart Effects on ISR-as-Reward}
\label{sec:goodhart}

We reverse ISR's role from observer to reward: $r = r_{\text{exec}}\cdot(1 + \beta\,\text{ISR})$, with matched random control ($u\sim\mathcal{U}(0,1)$).
The headline result is negative: our deconfounding analysis shows ISR's apparent $3.2\times$ advantage collapses to $1.06\times$ after controlling for premise count---a simple length penalty may achieve comparable effects.
Combined with the Goodhart effects documented below, this is evidence that component-necessity metrics should remain diagnostics, not optimization targets.

\paragraph{Multiplicative coupling.}
The reward $r_{\text{exec}}\cdot(1{+}\beta\,\text{ISR})$ couples structural and execution signals, preventing full separation of ISR gaming from execution-reward amplification.
Three observations constrain this confound: (i)~exec-only (\S\ref{sec:diagnostic}) degrades to $0\%$ non-hacking without ISR, so execution reward alone drives collapse; (ii)~the qualitative shift in hacking composition---TS from $0\%$ (exec-only) to $71\%$ under ISR---cannot be attributed to exec amplification, which preserves mode distribution; (iii)~an ISR-only condition is conceptually pathological, since without execution feedback models cannot learn provable formalizations.

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\resizebox{\columnwidth}{!}{%
\begin{tabular}{lcccccc}
\toprule
\textbf{Condition} & $\boldsymbol{\beta}$ & \textbf{PR\%}$\uparrow$ & \textbf{ISR}$^*$ & \textbf{TS\%} & \textbf{CE\%} & \textbf{N-H\%} \\
\midrule
Base (0-shot) & --- & 12 & --- & --- & --- & --- \\ %% registry: sta-phase0-baseline
SFT & --- & 13 & 0.595 & 0 & 8 & \textbf{92} \\ %% registry: plan009-sft-baseline-s42-1000/s43-1000/s44-1000-r3 (3-seed pooled)
\midrule
Exec-only$^\dagger$ & --- & 93 & 0.132 & 0 & 87 & 8 \\ %% registry: plan001-exec-only-s42-r2 (PG corrected)
ISR & 0.1 & 77{\scriptsize$\pm$14} & $\ddagger$ & \textbf{71}$^\P$ & 18 & 6 \\ %% registry: plan002-additive-b01-s42/s43-r2/s44 (pooled, D095 FINAL)
Random & 0.1 & \textbf{87}{\scriptsize$\pm$8} & 0.117{\scriptsize$\pm$.04} & 14$^\S$ & 71 & 10 \\ %% registry: plan018-additive-random-b01-s42/s43-r3/s44-r2 (D095 FINAL)
\midrule
ISR & 0.3 & 66{\scriptsize$\pm$42} & 0.39{\scriptsize$\pm$.31} & \textbf{50} & 37 & 11 \\ %% registry: plan002-additive-b03-s42/s43/s44 (pooled; D106 s43 genuine 23→20)
Random & 0.3 & 54{\scriptsize$\pm$35} & 0.09{\scriptsize$\pm$.09} & 7 & 91 & 2 \\ %% registry: plan017-additive-random-s42, exp1-random-b03-s43-r4, exp1-random-b03-s44-r2 (pooled; D086 TS: s42=4,s43=7,s44=0)
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}}
\caption{Goodhart landscape across reward conditions. 3-seed rows: TS/CE/non-hacking percentage (N-H) pooled across seeds; PR/ISR$^*$: mean$\pm$std of per-seed values. $^\dagger$Seed~42. $\ddagger$$\text{ISR}^*$ omitted for ISR $\beta{=}0.1$ because the raw mean is inflated by TS gaming; premise-count-matched value is $1.06\times$ random (\S\ref{sec:goodhart}, Appendix~\ref{app:detailed-results}). $^\P$Includes TS classifier false-negative correction (s44) and PG premise-subset gaming (s42; 5.2\% of proved; Appendix~\ref{app:h8}). $^\S$Random TS driven by seed~43 (38.3\%); seeds~42 and~44: 0\%. Per-seed breakdown in Appendix Table~\ref{tab:fol-detailed}.}
\label{tab:goodhart-landscape}
\end{table}

\textbf{Low-dose ISR ($\beta{=}0.1$): ISR-amplified gaming.}
TS tautological collapse occurs under both ISR and random conditions, but ISR amplifies it ${\sim}5\times$: $70.6\%$ ($163/231$) under ISR vs.\ $13.7\%$ ($36/262$) under random (Fisher $p{=}1.3{\times}10^{-39}$, $\text{OR}{=}15.05$, 95\% CI $[9.6, 23.6]$; all primary comparisons survive Bonferroni correction, Appendix~\ref{app:statistical-details}). %% registry: plan002 vs plan018 all seeds; D095 FINAL
Random TS is highly seed-dependent: $38.3\%$ in seed~43 vs.\ $0\%$ in seeds~42 and~44; ISR makes TS near-deterministic ($2/3$ seeds at $97$--$100\%$, Table~\ref{tab:fol-detailed}).
TS---a single premise identical to the conclusion, giving $\text{ISR}\geq 0.5$ by construction (\S\ref{sec:taxonomy-method})---is the canonical Goodhart move at the meta level: the model satisfies the LOO definition directly rather than producing a derivation that happens to satisfy it.
Total conclusion embedding ($\text{CE}+\text{TS}$) is GRPO-universal at $68$--$100\%$ across both conditions (pooled: ISR $89\%$, random $85\%$).
The composition differs: CE alone accounts for $71\%$ under random vs.\ $18\%$ under ISR---ISR converts CE into TS rather than reducing total embedding (Figure~\ref{fig:hacking-composition}).
Random also yields higher and more stable PR ($87{\pm}8\%$ vs.\ $77{\pm}14\%$; Cohen's $d{=}0.88$, $n{=}3$ per group).

\textbf{Premise-count confound.}
The sole non-TS ISR seed (s42) shows raw $\text{ISR}^{*}{=}0.415$, $3.2\times$ random ($0.131$)---a premise-minimization artifact ($n{=}5.58$ vs.\ $8.46$); matched comparison gives $1.06\times$ (Appendix~\ref{app:detailed-results}). %% D051 premise-count-matched analysis
After reclassifying PG and TS false negatives, non-hacking rates ($6.1\%$ ISR vs.\ $9.5\%$ random) are not significant ($p{=}0.182$; Appendix~\ref{app:h8}). %% D095 FINAL Fisher test
The key asymmetry is compositional: ISR converts stochastic TS into near-deterministic convergence ($2/3$ seeds ${\geq}97\%$), while random remains seed-dependent and CE-dominated.

