\caption{Training ISR trajectories across reward conditions. ISR $\beta{=}0.1$ seeds rise rapidly toward $\text{ISR}{\approx}1$ (Goodhart gaming via TS tautology); exec-only s44's plateau at ${\sim}0.5$ is an TSa artifact ($n{=}2$ gives $\text{ISR}{\geq}0.5$ by construction), not genuine structural quality; random $\beta{=}0.1$ remains low. The s42 1k-step trace shows ISR eventually saturates at $0.88$ through the same TS mechanism.}
\label{fig:training-isr-curves}
\end{figure}

\textbf{Temporal collapse.}
ISR $\beta{=}0.1$ non-hacking is transient (Figure~\ref{fig:training-isr-curves}): seed~42 drops $17.3\%{\to}2.2\%$ by 1000 steps as TS escalates to $96.7\%$---mirroring exec-only's ${\sim}0\%$ non-hacking despite opposite ISR ($0.88$ vs.\ $0.003$). %% registry: plan002-b01-s42-1000, plan016-extended-1000-s42

\textbf{Higher doses ($\beta{\geq}0.2$).}
At $\beta{=}0.2$, 3/3 seeds converge to TSa with $0\%$ non-hacking ($n{=}290$). %% registry: plan002-additive-b02-s42/s43/s44
At $\beta{=}0.3$, both ISR ($\text{PR}{=}66{\pm}42\%$) and random ($54{\pm}35\%$) destabilize training: ISR yields multi-modal failure (reward collapse, CE, pure TS across seeds;~\citealt{gao-reward-overoptimization}), random converges to CE ($91\%$, ${\leq}4\%$ non-hacking; ISR vs.\ random TS $\text{OR}{=}13.7$; Appendix~\ref{app:beta03}). %% registry: plan002-additive-b03-s42/s43/s44

\subsection{Cross-Domain Generalization}
\label{sec:crossdomain}
\label{sec:crosslogic}
\label{sec:crossdataset}

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.95\columnwidth]{figures/fig4_prolog_comparison.pdf}
\caption{Cross-logic results. \textbf{Left}: FOL vs.\ Prolog exec-only $\text{ISR}^*$ across 3 seeds (s44 Prolog $0.387$ is a small-sample artifact, $n{=}21$). \textbf{Right}: Prolog ISR-reward gaming-mode composition---all 3 seeds reach 0\% non-hacking through distinct mechanisms.}
\label{fig:prolog-comparison}
\end{figure}

\textbf{Prolog: universal Goodhart.}
We replicate the FOL pipeline with Prolog/SWI-Prolog (Figure~\ref{fig:prolog-comparison}).
Exec-only seeds show $\text{ISR}^*{\approx}0$ via redundant ground-fact coding (2/3 seeds); ISR flags this mode as it flags FOL's conclusion embedding.
Under ISR-as-reward, 3/3 seeds reach 0\% non-hacking through distinct mechanisms---clean TS (s42), uppercase pseudo-Prolog bypassing text-match classifiers (s43, all 38 proved at $n_{\text{necessary}}{=}1$), and query-as-fact gaming (s44, 95/97 at $n_{\text{necessary}}{=}1$; Table~\ref{tab:prolog-detailed}).
Prolog's simpler semantics broadens the gaming basin, eliminating the non-hacking residual entirely.

\textbf{ProofFOL exec-only: structural degeneration generalizes, though mode distributions differ.}
Two converged seeds ($\text{ISR}^*{<}0.07$) show 0--1.4\% genuine versus ${\sim}$8\% on FOLIO; s42 exhibits 100\% TS---absent from all FOLIO exec-only seeds---showing hacking-mode repertoire is dataset-specific.
All seeds have $\text{ISR}^*{<}0.12$, far below the baseline ($0.31$), consistent with the tautological attractor being metric-intrinsic.

\textbf{ProofFOL ISR ($\beta{=}0.1$): beneficial instability.}
ISR bonus reduces stable convergence from 2/3 to 0/3 seeds---superficially, another optimization failure.
Hacking-mode classification reverses this reading: ISR seeds average 76.8\% genuine versus 17.4\% for exec-only (permutation $p{=}0.10$, $N{=}3$), with s42 and s44 achieving 100\% genuine despite training collapse (Table~\ref{tab:prooffol-detailed}).
The models that failed to converge produced structurally honest proofs.

\textbf{Mechanism.}
Eval-time ISR confirms the asymmetry: on ProofFOL, hacking constructions yield $\text{ISR}{=}0.0$ while genuine proofs yield $\text{ISR}{=}0.27$ (s42) and $0.20$ (s44)---ISR correctly penalizes shortcuts.
On FOLIO (baseline $\text{ISR}^*{\approx}0.50$), $P{\to}P$ yields $\text{ISR}{\geq}0.5$, so ISR erroneously rewards tautological hacking (regressional Goodhart~\citep{manheim-goodhart-taxonomy}); on ProofFOL (baseline $\text{ISR}^*{=}0.31$), hacking does not satisfy ISR, so the bonus correctly suppresses shortcuts at the cost of convergence stability.
The same metric produces opposite outcomes---Goodhart collapse where hacking satisfies it (FOLIO), beneficial instability where it does not (ProofFOL)---determined by the dataset's hacking geometry.

